gaps_vs_v5_spec.gap:
There is no cross-engine, versioned registry of composable causal primitives. Primitives are private to each engine: scm `_Draft.blk_*` methods, abm boolean rule flags in `_KINDS`, techsys `_scn_*` builders. Engines are versioned only by `Engine.version='1'` and the contract by 'mech/1'.

Missing as reusable primitives:
- missing observations (readings are never dropped);
- selection effects;
- learning-driven behavioural change;
- adaptive learning opponents (abm incumbents follow fixed rules of thumb);
- explicit hysteresis (only emergent in tech_cascade);
- price-as-quality-signal (the spec's 'lower prices reduce perceived value' example).

check_mechanism lacks four of the required checks:
- hidden-answer leakage scan (only tests/redteam check that rig endpoints do not expose config);
- distinctness from existing training and evaluation worlds;
- identifiability for a stated experimental question (discoverability is an SNR on net only, not on causal edges);
- trivial dominant strategies beyond the two corners.

`admit` is module-level only: no economics checks and no scan for hidden-config keys. Non-mech world families (open_*, ow_*) have no per-world validity analogue.

Missing pieces:
- no versioned registry of objectives, participant policy distributions, action interfaces or difficulty levels;
- families carry no version;
- the only sampler is research.tasks.train_stream, for mech tasks only;
- train_stream does not reject val/test families;
- generate() will build any test-split world family on request (sealing exists only for task seeds via research.sealed).

The clarification replaces the 'CAM-compatible sampler' requirement with an architecture-independent one.

tests.covers:
scm engine:
- determinism and JSON round trip, purity, every edge function, pure delay, stock, feedback fixed point, regime switch, economics;
- motif census within declared motifs, structural families with unseen motifs, compositional families as unseen co-occurrences, training families structurally distinct;
- seeds draw different graphs (the `_shape` digest), opacity, calibration, boundedness and conservation;
- truth vs measured effects, confounder misleads passive correlation, regime optimum changes, two-seat coupling.

abm engine:
- contract, opacity, honest calibration register, purity, garbage settings, money conservation and budgets;
- per-family mechanism behaviour: awareness, WOM along edges, capacity rationing, trust gate, network effects, segments, bundles, attention pool;
- incumbent adaptation, exit and entry;
- truth reachability, lags and signs vs CRN interventions;
- calibration over 20 seeds, causal-story predictions, strategic structure of two-seat families, throughput >= 1000 ticks per CPU-second at N=500.

techsys engine:
- contract, opacity, host hooks (income, claims, constructive optimum), visible-gauge differences between families;
- purity, exact job and value conservation, revenue bound, state bounded for 2000 ticks;
- truth graph well-formed, implied monotone effects hold, start-up lags, non-monotone responses, thrash, batch knee, distractors, deadlines, arrivals, routing, cache;
- calibration over 20 seeds and per-scenario behaviour (contention, metastable cascade, redundant flip, lostwork, handoff, shared-pool commons), throughput >= 3000 ticks per CPU-second.

rig module and sim:
- TickRng fast path equals tick_rng; clip_value equals Lever.clip; MechSim snapshot and pickle; BatchSim equals individual sims;
- hosted world equals the simulator tick for tick (5 scm families); throughput bounds; demand-budget shortfall; fallback and idle;
- replay, regeneration and forks in both state modes; any engine hosted through the contract alone (toy engine); every importable engine hosted identically;
- seats, reservations, delegation grants, set validation, spec contents, secrecy of the mechanism, hypotheses stored internally;
- metrics, invariants, admission harness pass, bad config refused, world families for every mechanism family, overrides, a deployed controller program (sandbox-marked).

evaluator and validity:
- effect matrix equals the implied signs and lags on hand-made chains; effect sizes with standard errors;
- claim scoring, prediction error, discriminate, oracle finds the known optimum and beats defaults and random, regime-following oracle, regret, information efficiency, claims filed in a world scored outside it;
- world code does not import the evaluator;
- validity accepts generated mechanisms, checks dynamism, rejects broken engines for the right reason, solvability semantics, deterministic redraw, acceptance summary.
